1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,207
Splitting a bill with friends.

2
00:00:02,207 --> 00:00:03,261
Sharing a pizza.

3
00:00:03,261 --> 00:00:05,866
Counting pocket money
at the end of the week.

4
00:00:06,266 --> 00:00:10,971
Each time, you ask one question: did
I end up with more, or less, than

5
00:00:10,971 --> 00:00:12,116
I started with?

6
00:00:12,566 --> 00:00:15,986
Aarav draws carbon dioxide
two ways in his notebook.

7
00:00:15,986 --> 00:00:17,590
Both have full octets.

8
00:00:17,590 --> 00:00:19,043
Which one is right?

9
00:00:19,392 --> 00:00:23,015
Chemists answer with a simple
count, called formal charge.

10
00:00:23,515 --> 00:00:27,713
By the end, you will find the formal
charge of any atom, and use it to

11
00:00:27,713 --> 00:00:29,541
pick the best Lewis structure.

12
00:00:30,191 --> 00:00:31,331
First, a picture.

13
00:00:31,331 --> 00:00:33,289
You start the week with six coins.

14
00:00:33,639 --> 00:00:36,172
Coins in your own pocket are all yours.

15
00:00:36,172 --> 00:00:39,933
Coins in a jar you share with
a friend are only half yours.

16
00:00:40,333 --> 00:00:44,111
At the end, compare what you
started with and what you have now.

17
00:00:44,111 --> 00:00:46,397
The difference is like a formal charge.

18
00:00:46,797 --> 00:00:49,984
For an atom, the starting coins
are its valence electrons.

19
00:00:49,984 --> 00:00:51,391
Lone pairs are its own.

20
00:00:51,391 --> 00:00:53,560
Bond electrons are split half and half.

21
00:00:54,210 --> 00:00:56,462
Here is the formula, step by step.

22
00:00:56,812 --> 00:00:57,712
Step one.

23
00:00:57,430 --> 00:00:59,640
V, the valence electrons of the free atom.

24
00:01:00,390 --> 00:01:01,290
Step two.

25
00:01:01,054 --> 00:01:03,556
L, the electrons in
lone pairs on that atom.

26
00:01:03,556 --> 00:01:05,272
All of them count as its own.

27
00:01:06,022 --> 00:01:06,922
Step three.

28
00:01:06,802 --> 00:01:09,199
B, the electrons in the bonds around it.

29
00:01:09,199 --> 00:01:12,003
The atom gets only half,
so we use B over two.

30
00:01:12,753 --> 00:01:13,711
Step four.

31
00:01:13,711 --> 00:01:17,618
Formal charge equals V,
minus L, minus B over two.

32
00:01:18,368 --> 00:01:23,076
Let's try it on our molecule bench,
with ozone, O three, drawn as in NCERT.

33
00:01:23,426 --> 00:01:28,275
The middle oxygen has one lone pair, a
double bond on the left, and a single

34
00:01:28,275 --> 00:01:29,621
bond on the right.

35
00:01:30,021 --> 00:01:31,184
Middle oxygen.

36
00:01:31,184 --> 00:01:33,979
Six, minus two, minus half of six.

37
00:01:33,979 --> 00:01:34,879
Plus one.

38
00:01:35,271 --> 00:01:37,733
Left oxygen, with the double bond.

39
00:01:37,733 --> 00:01:40,460
Six, minus four, minus half of four.

40
00:01:40,460 --> 00:01:41,360
Zero.

41
00:01:41,432 --> 00:01:43,919
Right oxygen, with the single bond.

42
00:01:43,919 --> 00:01:46,517
Six, minus six, minus half of two.

43
00:01:46,517 --> 00:01:47,417
Minus one.

44
00:01:47,796 --> 00:01:50,621
Add them: plus one, zero, minus one.

45
00:01:50,621 --> 00:01:51,521
Zero.

46
00:01:51,192 --> 00:01:54,485
That matches ozone, which
has no overall charge.

47
00:01:55,135 --> 00:01:56,241
Side by side.

48
00:01:56,241 --> 00:02:01,134
A real charge means a whole electron
really moved, as in sodium chloride.

49
00:02:01,134 --> 00:02:04,041
A formal charge is only a count on paper.

50
00:02:04,441 --> 00:02:05,575
Pause and think.

51
00:02:05,575 --> 00:02:09,465
Does the middle oxygen in ozone
really carry a whole plus charge?

52
00:02:12,665 --> 00:02:13,565
No!

53
00:02:13,080 --> 00:02:15,123
Formal charge is a counting tool.

54
00:02:15,123 --> 00:02:17,834
It does not show a real
charge on the atom.

55
00:02:18,484 --> 00:02:19,473
Remember this.

56
00:02:19,473 --> 00:02:22,838
The best Lewis structure has
the smallest formal charges.

57
00:02:23,538 --> 00:02:24,734
Now, Aarav's puzzle.

58
00:02:24,734 --> 00:02:25,638
Carbon dioxide.

59
00:02:25,938 --> 00:02:29,262
Structure one: O double
bond C double bond O.

60
00:02:29,262 --> 00:02:32,585
Structure two: O triple
bond C single bond O.

61
00:02:33,085 --> 00:02:37,177
In structure one, each oxygen:
six, minus four, minus two.

62
00:02:37,177 --> 00:02:38,077
Zero.

63
00:02:37,730 --> 00:02:40,451
Carbon: four, minus zero, minus four.

64
00:02:40,451 --> 00:02:41,351
Zero.

65
00:02:41,754 --> 00:02:46,873
In structure two, the triple bonded
oxygen: six, minus two, minus three.

66
00:02:46,873 --> 00:02:47,773
Plus one.

67
00:02:48,468 --> 00:02:52,300
The single bonded oxygen:
six, minus six, minus one.

68
00:02:52,300 --> 00:02:53,204
Minus one.

69
00:02:53,204 --> 00:02:54,803
Carbon is still zero.

70
00:02:55,553 --> 00:02:57,440
Structure one has all zeros.

71
00:02:57,440 --> 00:03:01,137
So O double bond C double bond
O is the better structure.

72
00:03:01,787 --> 00:03:02,896
One more check.

73
00:03:02,896 --> 00:03:07,070
The formal charges always add up to
the charge on the whole molecule or

74
00:03:07,070 --> 00:03:07,970
ion.

75
00:03:07,950 --> 00:03:09,627
Take the ammonium ion.

76
00:03:09,627 --> 00:03:12,560
Nitrogen has four bonds and no lone pair.

77
00:03:12,560 --> 00:03:14,881
Five, minus zero, minus four.

78
00:03:14,881 --> 00:03:15,781
Plus one.

79
00:03:16,146 --> 00:03:19,310
Each hydrogen: one, minus zero, minus one.

80
00:03:19,310 --> 00:03:20,210
Zero.

81
00:03:19,887 --> 00:03:23,185
The total is plus one,
the charge of the ion.

82
00:03:23,835 --> 00:03:25,297
Now, the exam trap.

83
00:03:25,647 --> 00:03:28,478
Students subtract all the
bonding electrons, not half.

84
00:03:28,878 --> 00:03:32,673
But each bond is shared, so
only half belongs to the atom.

85
00:03:32,673 --> 00:03:34,250
Always use B over two.

86
00:03:34,900 --> 00:03:36,190
Where is this asked?

87
00:03:36,190 --> 00:03:40,251
Expect questions on the formal charge
of a marked atom, and on which Lewis

88
00:03:40,251 --> 00:03:41,758
structure is more stable.

89
00:03:42,358 --> 00:03:43,637
Exam level now.

90
00:03:43,637 --> 00:03:48,613
In the nitrate ion, nitrogen has one
double bond and two single bonds to

91
00:03:48,613 --> 00:03:49,513
oxygen.

92
00:03:49,328 --> 00:03:54,119
Find the formal charges on nitrogen,
and on a single bonded oxygen.

93
00:03:54,119 --> 00:03:55,453
Pause and solve.

94
00:04:00,853 --> 00:04:02,182
The answer is A.

95
00:04:02,182 --> 00:04:05,522
Plus one on nitrogen,
minus one on that oxygen.

96
00:04:06,022 --> 00:04:09,412
Nitrogen: five, minus
zero, minus half of eight.

97
00:04:09,412 --> 00:04:10,312
Plus one.

98
00:04:10,723 --> 00:04:14,269
Single bonded oxygen:
six, minus six, minus one.

99
00:04:14,269 --> 00:04:15,169
Minus one.

100
00:04:15,668 --> 00:04:21,004
Check: plus one, minus one, minus one,
and zero for the double bonded oxygen.

101
00:04:21,004 --> 00:04:23,362
Minus one, the charge of nitrate.

102
00:04:23,962 --> 00:04:25,502
Here is your recap.

103
00:04:25,502 --> 00:04:27,085
Screenshot this card.

104
00:04:30,285 --> 00:04:32,470
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

105
00:04:32,870 --> 00:04:33,770
One.

106
00:04:33,351 --> 00:04:35,622
Write the formula for formal charge.

107
00:04:36,422 --> 00:04:37,322
Two.

108
00:04:36,887 --> 00:04:40,268
What is the formal charge on
the middle oxygen of ozone?

109
00:04:41,068 --> 00:04:41,968
Three.

110
00:04:41,671 --> 00:04:44,966
Which carbon dioxide
structure is better, and why?

111
00:04:45,866 --> 00:04:49,550
Next time: some atoms are happy with
fewer than eight electrons, and some

112
00:04:49,550 --> 00:04:50,450
hold more.

113
00:04:50,235 --> 00:04:51,135
How?

114
00:04:50,637 --> 00:04:53,030
We find out in limitations
of the octet rule.
